WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:04.990
The day Sony Pictures, Disney, and Marvel
Studios' Spider-Man deal came to an end is

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.380
one that will live on in infamy.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.100
The news was and remains a huge deal.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.719
Since Sony and Disney, the parent company
of Marvel Studios, were unable to renegotiate

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:18.860
the terms of their original 2015 deal that
saw Marvel producing Spider-Man movies with

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:23.670
Sony retaining ownership, Tom Holland's Spider-Man
is out of the Marvel Cinematic Universe, potentially

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:24.200
for good.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.760
Fans the world over were shocked upon hearing
the news, and it's no exaggeration to say

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.580
that the response to Spider-Man leaving the
MCU has been emotional.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.580
Pop culture junkie, comic book enthusiast,
and filmmaker Kevin Smith took to Twitter

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:41.140
to beg Sony to agree to Disney's terms and
keep Spider-Man in the MCU:

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:42.670
"NOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO!!!!!!!!

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:43.670
Just stop it!

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:47.399
Please, @SonyPictures - give @Disney what
they want and many millions of us will keep

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:47.920
giving you our money!"

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:53.540
Smith's tweet was in reference to the reports
that Disney requested a 50 percent co-financing

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:58.220
share in the Spider-Man film franchise, and
asked to retain producing status on future

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.820
films, credited to Marvel Studios president
Kevin Feige.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.040
Sony is said to have flat-out refused the
proposition, as there was evidently no way

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.740
the studio would be comfortable with increasing
Marvel's current five percent cut of the franchise's

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.720
first-day profits to a 50 percent stake.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:16.660
On that note, Uproxx senior entertainment
writer Mike Ryan argued that Sony is in the

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.420
right for walking away, as it would be unreasonable
to expect the studio to bump up Disney's share

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.120
from five percent to 50 percent.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:24.820
Ryan posted on Twitter,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.380
"People seem to be mad at Sony for Spider-Man
and the MCU separating?

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.140
If you read the Deadline piece it sounds like
Disney raised its price from 5 percent to

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.020
50 percent, which is a lot!

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.340
It's not insane Sony didn't agree to that."

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.440
"I'll pay you the usual rate."

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.180
Ryan wasn't the only one who felt this way.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.760
Marvel fan @AhmedMansoor_ tweeted that Sony
was smart not to bend to Disney's whims, given

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.280
that the studio's Spidey franchise has been
flourishing as of late.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.120
Spider-Man: Far From Home recently became
Sony's highest-earning film of all time, its

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:01.550
2018 animated flick Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse
is an Academy Award-winner, and its Venom

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:07.550
franchise kicked off with an impressive $856
million worldwide pull, which recently motivated

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.340
Sony to greenlight a sequel with director
Andy Serkis.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.180
The user wrote,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.340
"It all sums up to money and greed.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.820
I mean, did Disney really think that Sony
would let them share 50/50 in revenue?

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:19.980
10/90 max maybe?

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.920
It just became their highest-grossing movie
of the all-time.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.860
With Venom &amp; Into the spiderverse proceeds
it's their gold mine now."

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.280
The Twitter account for Fandom.com joked that
Feige must have had the same conversation

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.820
with Disney CEO Bob Iger that Tony Stark had
with Steve Rogers at the start of Avengers:

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:36.340
Endgame.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.800
In the film, Tony told Steve that he "lost
the kid," referring to the fact that Peter

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:45.160
Parker got dusted in Thanos' Avengers: Infinity
War snap and there was nothing Tony could

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.340
do to save him.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.740
Though Disney apparently did try to keep Holland's
Spider-Man in the MCU, Sony pulled him into

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:52.560
its web in the end.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:54.600
"I don't wanna go…"

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.420
On the subject of comparing Sony to Thanos,
many feel the studio has a lot in common with

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.260
the intergalactic tyrant for not continuing
to negotiate with Disney and Marvel.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:15.180
Countless others are concerned that Spider-Man's
exit from the MCU means that the hero's relationship

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.140
with Tony Stark, not to mention the ending
of Far From Home, won't be followed up on.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:20.820
One user wrote on Twitter,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.940
"How does Sony not understand that they can't
just erase EVERYTHING that has happened in

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:25.580
endgame?

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.340
What do they think they're Thanos now?!

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.220
Snap and you can just erase spidey from existence
and everything he's been through with Tony??!!

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:33.520
NOPE #SaveSpiderMan."

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:34.780
Another added,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.580
"So Sony has replaced Thanos as the primary
villain of the MCU?"

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.550
Many other fans, like Twitter user @BOSSKyJo,
all but shouted their outrage.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:44.080
As that user put it,

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:49.720
"TONY STARK DIDNT DIE TO SAVE PETER PARKER
FOR SONY TO TAKE HIM OUT OF THE MCU!

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:50.240
#SaveSpiderMan."

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:55.240
Things went from bad to worse when Sony issued
its response to the deal's apparent collapse.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.880
A company spokesperson stated,

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.440
"We are disappointed, but respect Disney's
decision not to have [Kevin Feige] continue

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.540
as a lead producer of our next live-action
Spider-Man film.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.409
We hope this might change in the future, but
understand that the many new responsibilities

00:04:08.409 --> 00:04:12.819
that Disney has given him, including all their
newly added Marvel properties, do not allow

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.080
time for him to work on IP they do not own."

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.240
One fan expressed hope for some kind of agreement
to be reached in the future, directly addressing

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:21.900
Sony on Twitter with a post that reads,

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.320
"I respectfully request that you return to
the negotiating table and make a new deal

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.640
that will please us, the fans."

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.460
For our part, the only hope we have for Sony's
two forthcoming Spider-Man movies is that

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.220
whatever they do, they just don't make us
watch Uncle Ben die again.

